{"pdf_page": 53, "book_page": 41, "items": [{"label": "code", "text": "NHNO:(s)aq NH4(aq) + NO3-(aq) soH = +25.8kJ mol-1 △Ssystem = S[NH4+(aq)] + S[NO3-(aq)] - S[NH4NO3(s)] =+113.4 + 146.4 - 151.1J K-1 mol-1 = +108.7J K-1 mol-1 Hsol +25800 △S =-86.6JK-1mol-1 surroundings T 298 Stotal =(+108.7 - 86.6) = +22.1 J K-1 mol-1", "bbox": [65.2, 737.4, 314.0, 623.9]}, {"label": "text", "text": "Since △Stotal is positive, the dissolving of ammonium nitrate in water at 298K is thermodynamically spontaneous.Since the activation energyfor this processis very low, ammonium chloride is soluble in water at 298K.", "bbox": [41.5, 616.8, 442.9, 582.1]}, {"label": "text", "text": "We are now in aposition to think about thesolubility inwater ofsome otherionic solids.", "bbox": [41.5, 575.8, 399.4, 563.9]}, {"label": "text", "text": "TableAshowstherelevantthermodynamicdataforsomesolidsatatemperatureof298K. Thevalueshavebeenquotedtothenearestwholenumber.", "bbox": [41.9, 557.2, 413.7, 534.0]}, {"label": "table", "text": "| IONICSOLID   |   soH/kJmol-1 |   Ssurounding/JK-1 mol-1 |   Ssystem/JK-1 mol-1 |   Stotal/JK-1mol-1 | SOLUBILITY   |\n|--------------|---------------|--------------------------|----------------------|--------------------|--------------|\n| NaCl         |            +4 |                      -13 |                  +43 |                +56 | soluble      |\n| NH,CI        |           +15 |                      -50 |                 +167 |               +117 | soluble      |\n| AgCI         |           +66 |                     -221 |                  +33 |               -188 | insoluble    |\n| MgSO4        |           -91 |                     +305 |                 -213 |                +92 | soluble      |\n| CuSO4        |           -73 |                     +245 |                 -192 |                +53 | soluble      |\n| CaSO4        |           -18 |                      +60 |                 -145 |                -85 | insoluble    |", "bbox": [41.4, 527.5, 473.4, 405.5]}, {"label": "text", "text": "tableA", "bbox": [41.5, 403.1, 71.4, 392.0]}, {"label": "section_header", "text": "REACTIONSINVOLVINGACHANGEINNUMBEROFMOLESFROM REACTANTSTOPRODUCTS", "bbox": [42.7, 377.8, 389.7, 350.2]}, {"label": "text", "text": "If you increase thenumber of moles that you have,you automatically increase thenumber of particles (i.e. atoms, molecules or ions) present. This will result in an increase in the number of ways that the particles can be arranged, and this increases the entropy of the system, making Ssystem positive.", "bbox": [40.0, 344.3, 447.5, 296.2]}, {"label": "text", "text": "We will now look again at the reactions shown above, but this time we will consider the change in the numberofmolesfromreactantstoproducts.", "bbox": [41.5, 291.1, 450.6, 267.4]}, {"label": "section_header", "text": "EXAMPLE1", "bbox": [41.9, 258.0, 92.8, 246.1]}, {"label": "text", "text": "2CHCOOH(1) + (NH4)CO3(S)", "bbox": [66.0, 239.0, 191.3, 226.4]}, {"label": "text", "text": "→ 2CH,COONH4(s) + H2O() + CO2(g)", "bbox": [85.0, 227.6, 247.6, 213.8]}, {"label": "text", "text": "Number of moles of reactants=3", "bbox": [41.9, 210.6, 183.2, 199.6]}, {"label": "text", "text": "Numberof moles ofproducts=4", "bbox": [42.3, 194.1, 181.6, 183.8]}, {"label": "text", "text": "So, ASsystem is positive.", "bbox": [40.0, 180.3, 133.9, 163.3]}, {"label": "section_header", "text": "EXAMPLE2", "bbox": [41.9, 157.8, 93.5, 146.0]}, {"label": "text", "text": "Ba(OH)2.8H2O(s) + 2NH,CI(s) →BaCl2(s) + 10H2O(l) + 2NH3(g)", "bbox": [66.0, 138.9, 224.3, 114.0]}, {"label": "text", "text": "Number of moles ofreactants=3", "bbox": [41.9, 110.5, 183.2, 100.2]}, {"label": "text", "text": "Number of moles of products =13", "bbox": [41.9, 94.7, 186.3, 83.7]}, {"label": "text", "text": "So, △Ssystem is positive.", "bbox": [41.1, 78.5, 132.4, 65.5]}]}